.TH "COMPLYCTL\-PROVIDER\-OPA" "1" "September 2026" "ComplyTime Manual"
.SH NAME
complyctl\-provider\-opa \- OPA/Conftest scanning provider for complyctl
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]complyctl\-provider\-opa\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]complyctl\-provider\-opa\f[R] is a gRPC plugin for
\f[B]complyctl\f[R](1) that provides compliance scanning capabilities
using the Open Policy Agent (OPA) engine via Conftest.
It is not invoked directly by users; \f[B]complyctl\f[R] discovers and
launches it automatically when the provider is installed.
.PP
The provider receives assessment configurations from \f[B]complyctl\f[R]
and evaluates configuration files and infrastructure\-as\-code against
Rego policies using the \f[B]conftest\f[R] CLI tool.
It supports both remote git repositories and local filesystem paths as
scan targets, and loads policy bundles from OCI registries or cached
complypack content.
.PP
Communication with \f[B]complyctl\f[R] uses the hashicorp/go\-plugin
gRPC interface, implementing three RPCs: \f[B]Describe\f[R],
\f[B]Generate\f[R], and \f[B]Scan\f[R].
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is provided through variables defined in assessment plan
targets.
These variables are passed to the provider via \f[B]complyctl\f[R] and
are not set manually by the user.
.TP
\f[B]url\f[R] (optional, mutually exclusive with \f[B]input_path\f[R])
HTTPS URL of a git repository to clone and scan.
One of \f[B]url\f[R] or \f[B]input_path\f[R] must be provided per
target.
Example: \f[B]https://github.com/org/repo\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]input_path\f[R] (optional, mutually exclusive with \f[B]url\f[R])
Local filesystem path to scan.
Must exist and must not contain path traversal sequences
(\f[B]../\f[R]).
One of \f[B]url\f[R] or \f[B]input_path\f[R] must be provided per
target.
.TP
\f[B]branches\f[R] (optional)
Comma\-separated list of branches to scan for remote repositories.
Must match the pattern \f[B][a\-zA\-Z0\-9._/\-]+\f[R].
Path traversal sequences are rejected.
Default: \f[B]main\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]access_token\f[R] (optional)
Authentication token for cloning private git repositories.
Used as the password in git credential helper configuration.
Rejected if it contains newlines or null bytes.
.TP
\f[B]scan_path\f[R] (optional)
Subdirectory within the cloned repository to scan.
Path traversal sequences (\f[B]../\f[R]) are rejected.
Default: root of the cloned repository.
.TP
\f[B]workspace\f[R] (injected by complyctl)
Workspace base directory.
The provider excludes this base joined with \f[B].complytime\f[R], as
well as its own artifact directory when different.
When absent, the provider uses its configured artifact directory.
.TP
\f[B]platform\f[R] (optional)
Platform hint for self\-hosted git instances (e.g., \f[B]github\f[R],
\f[B]gitlab\f[R]).
Affects the credential username (\f[B]oauth2\f[R] for GitLab,
\f[B]x\-access\-token\f[R] otherwise).
Default: auto\-detected from the URL hostname.
.TP
\f[B]opa_bundle_ref\f[R] (required when no complypack is available)
OCI reference for a Conftest policy bundle.
Used to pull policies via \f[B]conftest pull\f[R] when no complypack
content path is set by \f[B]complyctl\f[R].
Example: \f[B]oci://ghcr.io/org/policies:v1\f[R]
.PP
The OCI policy bundle must include a \f[B]complytime\-mapping.json\f[R]
file that maps requirement IDs to Rego namespaces.
The provider returns an error if this file is missing.
.SH SELECTING SCAN INPUT
Keep target files outside the complyctl workspace so generated artifacts
and prior results do not affect OPA assessments.
For a local directory target that contains a workspace, the provider
excludes the workspace files, including user\-owned files.
Files outside the excluded directories remain eligible subject to the
link and input checks below.
.PP
The provider resolves the host workspace as the \f[B]workspace\f[R] base
injected by complyctl joined with \f[B].complytime\f[R].
It also excludes its own artifact directory, normally
\f[B].complytime\f[R] relative to the provider\(cqs working directory,
when that directory differs.
For example, a host base of \f[I]/srv/team\f[R] and a provider working
directory of \f[I]/srv/scan\f[R] create two boundaries:
\f[I]/srv/team/.complytime\f[R] and \f[I]/srv/scan/.complytime\f[R].
If the host does not provide a workspace base, the provider uses its
configured artifact directory.
The provider resolves both paths through symbolic links.
If it cannot resolve a boundary, it rejects the target.
Check that both workspace directories exist and that the provider can
access their paths, then retry the scan.
.PP
The provider rejects an explicitly selected local workspace file or
directory.
To scan such a file, copy it to a directory outside both workspace
boundaries and set \f[B]input_path\f[R] to the copy.
Use a copy, not a hard link: the provider checks filesystem identity and
rejects a hard link to workspace content when it cannot exclude it
safely.
It also rejects unsafe symbolic links and unresolved paths.
.PP
An intentional remote clone remains eligible, even though it is stored
inside the workspace.
The provider rejects clone files hard\-linked to other workspace
content.
It also rejects a remote scan path or traversed link that reaches other
workspace content or matches its file identity.
The provider rejects unresolved paths and link cycles.
Non\-cyclic links within the selected clone remain eligible.
Fix or remove unsafe links, or copy the intended input outside the
workspace.
.PP
Local directories and remote clones or \f[B]scan_path\f[R] directories
with no confirmed evaluated input produce an operational error and no
assessments for that target.
This includes empty, workspace\-only, and fully ignored inputs.
Add a supported input file or adjust the effective ignore rule; other
targets retain their results.
.PP
For a remote target selecting multiple branches, an input\-safety
rejection or lack of confirmed evaluated directory input in any branch
suppresses assessments from every branch of that configured target.
This applies in either branch order.
The error identifies the rejected target and branch; other configured
targets retain their assessments.
Repair the rejected branch\(cqs input or select only the intended
eligible branches.
Previously written per\-branch result files are not deleted.
.PP
Workspace exclusion does not prune directories from traversal.
An unreadable directory or broken link can still cause an operational
error; fix the path or its permissions before retrying.
Preflight is not a sandbox: concurrent filesystem changes between
preflight and conftest traversal can defeat isolation.
.SS Conftest ignore configuration
For a local directory that contains a workspace, the provider combines a
nonempty \f[B]CONFTEST_IGNORE\f[R] with the workspace exclusion.
If the variable is empty or unset and an applicable conftest
configuration file exists, or the provider cannot determine whether one
exists, it rejects the target rather than discarding a possible user
ignore rule.
Non\-overlapping local targets and remote clones do not need this
workspace ignore rule.
.PP
To resolve this error, select a file or directory outside both workspace
boundaries that does not contain either boundary.
Alternatively, set \f[B]CONFTEST_IGNORE\f[R] to the effective ignore
expression, including the file\(cqs existing \f[B]ignore\f[R] value.
If the file defines no ignore rule, use a valid expression that matches
no file paths:
.IP
.EX
export CONFTEST_IGNORE=\(aq\(ha$\(aq
.EE
.PP
Other conftest configuration settings continue to apply.
The provider checks configuration in conftest\(cqs working directory and
resolves a relative \f[B]CONFTEST_CONFIG_FILE\f[R] from that directory.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]CONFTEST_IGNORE\f[R]
Conftest ignore regular expression.
For local directory targets that contain a workspace, the provider
combines a nonempty value with the workspace exclusion.
See \f[B]Conftest ignore configuration\f[R] above.
.TP
\f[B]CONFTEST_CONFIG_FILE\f[R]
Path to the conftest configuration file.
The provider resolves a relative path from conftest\(cqs working
directory when checking for a configuration that may define an ignore
rule.
.TP
\f[B]COMPLYTIME_MACHINE_ID_FILE\f[R]
Path to the machine\-id file used for host identification in evidence
remarks.
The file content is included in each Evidence.Source.Remarks field
alongside the hostname so auditors can identify the host where the scan
was executed.
Default: \f[B]/etc/machine\-id\f[R]
.SH EXTERNAL TOOLS
Requires the following tools at runtime:
.TP
\f[B]conftest\f[R]
Open Policy Agent test runner for structured data.
Install conftest and make it available on \f[B]PATH\f[R].
The provider uses behavior\-based compatibility rather than an
exact\-version runtime check.
It does not require successful version detection or embedded module
metadata; a source build reporting \f[B]dev\f[R] is not rejected for
that reason.
Input\-safety, execution, and structured\-output validation still apply.
.RS
.PP
The real\-tool regression suite has been exercised against
\f[B]0.68.2\f[R] and \f[B]0.71.1\f[R].
It runs against any installed executable, without skipping based on
version.
These runs establish tested behavior, not compatibility with every
future release.
Local CI does not install conftest, so recurring changing\-release
coverage remains a tracked follow\-up: \c
.UR https://github.com/complytime/complyctl/issues/957
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
To record the installed version when diagnosing an issue:
.IP
.EX
conftest \-\-version
.EE
.PP
Releases: \c
.UR https://github.com/open-policy-agent/conftest/releases
.UE \c
.RE
.TP
\f[B]git\f[R]
Required for cloning remote repositories when \f[B]url\f[R] is
specified.
Provided by the \f[B]git\f[R] Fedora package.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I].complytime/opa/\f[R]
Provider workspace directory.
All paths below are relative to the current working directory.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/opa/results/<target>\-<branch>.json\f[R]
Per\-target result JSON summarizing conftest findings for a single
target and branch combination.
For local targets, the branch component is omitted.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/opa/generated/<content\-key>/scan\-config.json\f[R]
Scan configuration written by the \f[B]Generate\f[R] RPC.
Records matched requirement IDs, the Rego\-to\-Gemara reverse mapping,
and the resolved policy directory path.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/opa/generated/generate\-index.json\f[R]
Maps each generated target to its content\-keyed scan configuration.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/opa/generated/complytime\-mapping.json\f[R]
Mapping file shipped in the OCI policy bundle or complypack.
Maps Gemara requirement IDs to Rego policy namespaces.
Required for requirement\-scoped evaluation.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/opa/repos/\f[R]
Directory for cloned git repositories when \f[B]url\f[R] targets are
used.
Each repository is cloned into a subdirectory named by the sanitized URL
and branch.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/opa/policy/\f[R]
Directory for downloaded OPA policy bundles (from OCI registries or
complypack content).
.SH EVIDENCE
The provider records an \f[B]Evidence\f[R] entry on each
\f[B]AssessmentLog\f[R] returned by the \f[B]Scan\f[R] RPC:
.TP
\f[B]Type\f[R]
\f[B]ConftestResult\f[R] \(em the conftest evaluation output for the
target input path.
.TP
\f[B]Production\f[R]
The provider invokes \f[B]conftest test\f[R] with namespace\-scoped
policies against the input path (a cloned repository directory or a
local filesystem path).
The conftest JSON output is parsed into per\-target result files.
.TP
\f[B]EvaluationLog representation\f[R]
Each assessment log carries an Evidence entry with:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Source.Coordinate\f[R]: the input path evaluated by conftest (e.g.,
\f[I].complytime/opa/repos/<repo>/<branch>\f[R] or a local filesystem
path)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Source.ReferenceID\f[R]:
\f[B]conftest\-input\-<target>[\-<branch>]\f[R]
.PP
For regular\-file inputs, \f[B]Source.Digest\f[R] contains the SHA\-256
hash (\f[B]sha256:<hex>\f[R]) of the file content at scan time.
For directory inputs (cloned repositories), no digest is recorded.
.RE
.SH EXIT CODES
This provider is a gRPC subprocess managed by \f[B]complyctl\f[R].
Exit codes follow the hashicorp/go\-plugin protocol and are not
user\-facing.
